noble tradition|noble tradition / tradition of the nobles\nSanskrit: āryavaṃśa <Word/Term/Phrase>\n\n\nTibetan renderings of āryavaṃśa: {'phags rigs/}, {'phags pa'i rigs/}\n\nFour āryavaṃśa-s are distinguished in Buddhism, namely:\n(1) contentment with one’s monks’ robe,\n(2) contentment with one’s alms food, \n(3) contentment with one’s beds and seats, and\n(4) the state of finding delight in abandonment and meditative cultivation\nIn the 6th chapter of the Abhidharmakośabhāṣya, they are described in the beginning of the depiction of the way to salvation.\nāryavaṃśa (m.; = Pali ariyavaṃsa, see CPD s.v.), the (fourfold) attitudes (lit. ‘stocks’, sources) of the Buddhist saint, listed AbhidhK. LaV-P. vi.l46ff.: RP 13.17–18 caturṇām °śānām anuvartanatā; 14.7 caturāryavaṃśaniratā; KP\n 6.17 (vs) ājīvaśuddho sthita āryavaṃśe; 123.3 saṃtuṣṭaḥ caturbhir āryavaṃśair (the first three are contentment with garments, food, seat-and-bed of monks; acc. to AbhidhK. the fourth is devotion to the way to release, by appropriate behavior); 126.7 (vs) °vaṃśehi samanvito ’pi; Śikṣ 105.8 caturāryavaṃśaparivarjanatayā (due to lābhasatkāra; misunderstood by Bendall and Rouse); 191.10 °vaṃśa-saṃtuṣṭi (cf. above).\n\nSee the full entry at:https://www.itlr.net/hwid:220536
