going horizontally|“going horizontally,” an animal\nSanskrit: tiryañc <Word/Term/Phrase>\n\n\nTibetan renderings of tiryañc: {dud 'gro/}\n\nListed as the second of the sixty-four kinds of sentient beings in the Yogācārabhūmi.\nIn general, tiryañc (“animal”) is said to be one of the five or six forms of saṃsāric existence or destiny (gati), or one of the three lower forms of existence or destinies (durgati). All four types of birth, i.e., birth from a womb, egg, moisture, or spontaneous birth, are said to be possible in the case of animals. Some asuras as well as some powerful nāgas are included amongst the gods, whereas some asuras and most nāgas are said to be animals. Typically, animals suffer from ignorance, from preying upon each other, being enslaved and slaughtered.\n\nSee the full entry at:https://www.itlr.net/hwid:252975
