hell|hell, place of torment\nSanskrit: naraka <Word/Term/Phrase>\n\n\nTibetan renderings of naraka: {dmyal ba/}, {sems can dmyal ba/}, {ngan 'gro'i mthil/}\n\nIt appears that at least in Buddhist sources, naraka can occasionally mean  “inhabitant of hell.” This would clearly be the case if the manuscript reading narakāḥ in a list of groups of sentient beings (sattvanikāya) is not a secondary corruption of an original reading *nārakāḥ. Also in an Abhidharmakośabhāṣya passage (see below), naraka, if it is not a textual corruption, denotes the inhabitants of hell. The matter definitely requires more research.\n\nSee the full entry at:https://www.itlr.net/hwid:273398
